@h1 Node use & the bare reference h1@

Every structure in Wit is a @strong node strong@ — a name that wraps content.
This page is the atom the rest of the language builds on: how a node opens and
closes, where its name ends, and how the same handle reaches into data. Read it
before parameters, before definitions.

@h2 Opening, closing, and the body h2@

An opening handle is @@@code @name code@@@; the matching close is
@@@code name@ code@@@; the @strong body strong@ is everything between them.

@@@pre
@aside
A note set apart from the main text.
aside@
pre@@@

Scope comes from the open/close pair, never from indentation — you may indent
for your eye, but the parser only counts handles. Nodes nest, and two uses of
the same name pair @strong last-in-first-out strong@, like brackets:

@@@pre
@quote @cite Weil cite@ wrote of attention. quote@
pre@@@

The inner @@@code @cite … cite@ code@@@ closes before the outer
@@@code @quote … quote@ code@@@ (fixture 04-nodes-use/nested-same-name).

@h2 Block or inline is decided by position h2@

Whether a node renders as a block or inline is a @strong renderer decision from
position strong@, never something you mark. A node that stands alone on its
own line becomes a block; a node sitting inside a run of prose stays inline.

@@@pre
@aside
This is a block — it sits on its own line.
aside@

The lamp was @highlight still burning highlight@ at dawn.
pre@@@

The first is a block aside; the second keeps @@@code @highlight … highlight@
code@@@ inline in the sentence (fixtures 04-nodes-use/block-name-body,
inline-name-body).

@h2 The bare reference h2@

A handle with no body and no closer is a @strong bare reference strong@ — a
pointer to a defined entity, resolved when the document expands. It is how you
cite named content mid-sentence.

@@@pre
Attention, as @weil argued, is a moral act.
pre@@@

Here @@@code @weil code@@@ stands in for whatever @@@code #weil code@@@ defines
(fixtures 04-nodes-use/bare-reference, bare-reference-adjacent-prose).

@h2 Where the handle ends h2@

The handle name is the character class @code [A-Za-z0-9_-] code@ — letters,
digits, underscore, and hyphen. The @strong first byte that is not in that
class ends the handle strong@. A leading dot instead opens a data-access path
(see below); anything else — a space, comma, or period — closes the name.

@@@pre
@paper-stats      ~ hyphen is part of the name
@chapter_one      ~ underscore is part of the name
@h2               ~ a trailing digit is fine
@weil, the writer ~ the comma ends the handle
pre@@@

These are fixtures 04-nodes-use/hyphenated-name, underscored-name,
numeric-suffix, and bare-reference. A following space or comma simply ends the
name and returns to prose (bare-reference-adjacent-prose).

@aside(class note) 💡 Handles are matched @strong exactly and case-sensitively strong@. @@@code @Weil code@@@ does not resolve @@@code #weil code@@@. Only data-access segments fuzzy-match — see the Data access guide. aside@

@h2 Access paths in prose h2@

Wherever a bare reference is legal, so is a @strong data-access path strong@:
@@@code @name.field code@@@ reaches into a record or collection and drops the
value straight into your sentence.

@@@pre
The title is @book.title for this edition.
pre@@@

The chain follows dots until it hits a byte that is not part of a segment — the
sentence-final period ends it cleanly (fixture 04-nodes-use/dotted-access). This
is the verified way to put data into prose; @@@code {{book.title}} code@@@ does
@strong not strong@ interpolate in a paragraph.

@h2 Empty bodies and nesting h2@

An empty pair @@@code @x x@ code@@@ is a real node with an @strong empty
children array strong@ — not null, and the single space between the markers is
not counted as a child (fixture 04-nodes-use/empty-body). Nesting has no depth
limit; the pairing is purely lexical.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li @strong Expecting a period to stay in the name. strong@ @@@code @weil.
code@@@ ends the handle at the period; the sentence keeps its full stop. Only a
dot @em followed by em@ an identifier opens an access path. li@
@li @strong Assuming the name is case-insensitive. strong@ @@@code @Weil
code@@@ and @@@code @weil code@@@ are different handles. li@
@li @strong Writing an email address in prose. strong@ A word immediately
followed by @@@code @ code@@@ — as in @@@code keeper@host code@@@ — reads as a
@em close em@ token and splits your paragraph. To show one literally, wrap it in
frozen code. See the Gotchas guide. li@
ul@

